<h2>The question</h2>

<p>The #3717 build wired the provider router into the prototype branch's dispatch path and stopped at five
choices the card had left open. <a href="/backlog/3801-decision-review-the-five-choices-the-3717-dispatch-routing-b/">#3801</a>
asks the operator to review them: which routing stage a code-change launch is, the three task types no dispatch
kind produces, whether role dispatches call the router, how an unsized card is read, and where the provider
override lives. The router's graduation model is ratified (<code>#delegation-trial-record-graduation</code>, from
#3690), and so is the vendor registry (<code>#agent-vendor-registry</code>, from #3658). The full working is in
<a href="/reports/2026-09-21-dispatch-routing-review-and-branch-health-grounding.md">the session report</a>.</p>

<h2>Measured on 2026-09-21, branch tip 5ab89f87b</h2>

<ul>
  <li>The branch's trial file has 18 records, none with a task type. The 26 delegation trials the router was
    designed around exist only on <code>main</code>.</li>
  <li>Fed <code>main</code>'s trials, the branch's own routing function sends a small <code>fix</code> to Codex and
    a conflict <code>fix</code> to Antigravity, both at an automatically promoted <code>spot-check</code>. The
    ratified rule says promotion takes an explicit act by the operator.</li>
  <li>An override to one provider records the supervision level another provider's trials earned.</li>
  <li>The record's <code>executed</code> field is the constant <code>claude</code>, even when the per-item
    <code>deliveryAgent:</code> marker makes Codex deliver.</li>
  <li>Tasks are unsized by the backlog's own rule: all 192 open tasks are unsized, and all 381 open stories are sized.</li>
  <li>The <code>spot-check</code> values come from the dispatch path's placeholder risk thresholds (low risk: a streak
    of 2 and no positive control). With the router's defaults, both triples stay at <code>full</code>.</li>
  <li>With supervision enforcement switched on, every code-change dispatch would be held, because no route names
    a supervisor.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Prior art</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>A default applies only when nothing is declared.</strong> Kubernetes' LimitRange: "the LimitRange
    admission controller applies default request and limit values for all Pods (and their containers) that do
    not set compute resource requirements" (kubernetes.io, Limit Ranges). The default is a policy value the
    cluster owner sets, not something inferred per request. That is the shape the prep lands on for an unsized
    task: a table row set from measured data, not a guess made at dispatch time.</li>
  <li><strong>The environment holds per-deploy configuration, not per-item choices.</strong> "The twelve-factor
    app stores config in environment variables" (12factor.net, Config). Config there is what varies between
    deploys. A routing override for one item is not that, which is why a process-wide variable is the wrong home
    for it.</li>
  <li><strong>A per-run override is declared and bound to the run.</strong> GitHub Actions'
    <code>workflow_dispatch</code> lets a workflow declare "custom-defined input properties, default input values,
    and required inputs", and the run receives them in its event payload (docs.github.com). The repo's
    per-item analogue already exists and is ratified: the <code>deliveryAgent:</code> marker in the item's own
    file, which lacks only the declared reason.</li>
</ul>

<h2>How the research reshaped the forks</h2>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Fork 1 (routing stage):</strong> keep <code>task</code>. The story stage forces Claude on every
    dispatch, so no route could leave Claude.</li>
  <li><strong>Fork 2 (unproduced task types):</strong> keep "none is produced". Re-using the <code>other</code>
    trial history is trial-data work, not a routing default.</li>
  <li><strong>Fork 3 (role dispatches):</strong> keep them outside the router. The reviewer seat is already
    governed by <code>#reviewer-tool-surface-and-containment</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Fork 4 (unsized cards):</strong> keep 900 lines. A task's estimate becomes a table row set later by
    a batched finding from measured task diffs. The skeptic refuted the first draft's 80-line read.</li>
  <li><strong>Fork 5 (the override):</strong> change it. The ratified <code>deliveryAgent:</code> marker, with a
    required reason, is the one override; both process-wide variables are retired.</li>
  <li><strong>Settled by statute:</strong> supervision is computed for the triple that runs, <code>executed</code>
    records the vendor spawned, and <code>routed</code> is never overwritten by an override.</li>
</ul>

